#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Per-cell Tier-3 qualification oracle for the acq v1 Python replacement.

Drives the SHIPPED CLI (``python -m acq_py``) as subprocesses against a real
msb or sbx backend and returns 0 (PASS), 2 (BLOCKED), or 1 (FAIL). A human, or
``python -m qualification.host``, runs it under the qualification harness, which
emits the verbatim ``RESULT`` transcript line (ADR-0044m):

    python -m qualification run --backend msb \\
        --backend-version "$(msb --version)" --os Linux --os-build … --arch … \\
        --hypervisor kvm --rev "$(git rev-parse HEAD)" --image IMG \\
        -- scripts/qualify-backend-cell --backend msb

The oracle itself only reports an exit status and never emits the ``RESULT``
line; that is the harness's job. What it checks, per ADR-0044m (oracle numbers
are the ADR's):

    * boot contract (3)   — create/ls/exec/rm round trip against the default
                            shell image. Every guest probe prints a number the
                            guest computes, so an echo of the command line is not
                            a pass.
    * credential binding  — ``provider-credential set`` binds a throwaway value
      and non-exposure (2)  before the sandbox is created; inside the guest the
                            bound variable must hold a non-empty placeholder, and
                            the value must not appear in the guest's environment
                            or PID 1's (an unreadable ``/proc/1/environ`` is a
                            reported gap: BLOCKED). ``secret set/ls/rm`` and
                            ``provider-credential set/ls/rm`` must never print the
                            value. Both backends: AGENTS.md documents a credential
                            path on msb (``--secret ENV@HOST``) as well as sbx; on
                            both, a non-built-in service is bound with
                            ``--host``/``--env``.
    * kit apply (3)       — the guest has the file the pinned usai-provider kit
                            places (``KIT_PROBE_FILE``).

Prerequisite (sbx): the usai-provider kit is selected, and acq refuses a create
whose provider key the backend cannot inject. On msb the oracle stores a
throwaway usai key in its private acq store; on sbx the key must be in sbx's
own proxy table, which the oracle never writes globally (that would overwrite
the operator's key). So sbx's global table must already hold a ``USAI_API_KEY``
custom secret for ``api.gsa.usai.gov`` (``acq secret set -g usai`` with the sbx
backend). The oracle checks ``sbx secret ls --json`` first and reports BLOCKED
(exit 2) when it is missing or unreadable.
    * read-only trusted   — msb only. In this cell's own guest: the trusted-config
      config mount (1)      mount point (``HOST_CONFIG_GUEST_DIR``) is listed with
                            ``ro`` in ``/proc/mounts``, a write to it (as root when
                            ``sudo -n`` works) fails with EROFS, and the host
                            directory acq mounts there is byte-identical
                            afterwards. acq's sandbox record must also declare the
                            mount (``ro_host_config``), and the committed canary
                            ``scripts/qualify-msb-ro-mount`` must pass; it shows,
                            in a sandbox of its own, that this msb keeps a ``:ro``
                            volume read-only even against remount attempts. Canary
                            exit 1 with its ``RESULT FAIL`` line is FAIL; exit 0
                            needs its ``RESULT PASS`` line; anything else, a
                            timeout, or no usable ``bash`` is BLOCKED.

Not covered here, because they need something an unattended run on one host
cannot provide: swap-on-the-wire substitution (an endpoint that reports the
credential it received), attach (a terminal), version fail-closed against a real
older backend (covered offline by the Tier-1 vectors), and remount attempts
inside this cell's own guest (the canary makes them in its own sandbox). See
docs/howto/qualification.md.

The control checks all run and every failure is printed (``FAIL: <step>:
<why>`` on stderr), so one run shows everything that is missing; a boot-contract
failure stops the run.

Isolation: the run must leave the operator's own acq configuration, state and
credentials exactly as found. Every acq subprocess gets a private configuration
and state tree (``XDG_CONFIG_HOME``, ``XDG_STATE_HOME``, ``XDG_DATA_HOME``,
``XDG_CACHE_HOME``, ``ACQ_STATE_DIR``, ``ACQ_SECRET_STORE_DIR``) that is deleted
afterwards, and every identifier that reaches a backend's own store (sandbox,
secret service, provider id, credential variables) carries a per-run nonce, so a
step can never overwrite or remove an existing binding. The names are printed to
stderr first, so a run that is killed outright can be cleaned up by hand.
``HOME`` is kept: the backend CLIs keep their login, daemon and image cache
there, and the oracle qualifies the operator's real backend. The caller's
``ACQ_*`` variables are not passed on, except those ending in ``_TIMEOUT``
(timing knobs such as ``ACQ_MSB_READY_TIMEOUT``): an ``ACQ_MSB_BIN=__stub__`` or
``ACQ_SECURITY_MOUNT_STATE`` would otherwise turn a live run into a stubbed one.
``PYTHONPATH`` is fixed to this checkout's ``py-acq/src``, the CLI runs under
``python -P -s``, and ``msb``/``sbx``/``bash`` are resolved by acq's own resolver
(``acq_py.hostexec``) from absolute ``PATH`` entries only, never the current
directory, and only as ``.exe`` files on Windows (there, never the WSL launcher
``System32\\bash.exe``; Git Bash is looked for in its install locations first). The canary honours only
``STEP_TIMEOUT``/``EXEC_TIMEOUT`` from the caller; its image is always the
default (``QUALIFY_IMAGE`` and ``EXPECT_SHA`` are removed, and
``ACQ_QUALIFY_OUT_DIR`` is an ``ACQ_*`` variable), and its sandbox gets this run's
nonce name (``QUALIFY_SANDBOX_NAME``), which teardown also removes.

Aborts: SIGTERM (and SIGBREAK on Windows) raise ``SystemExit`` and SIGINT
``KeyboardInterrupt``, so teardown runs; the first of them also swallows every
later one, so a second stop cannot cut the cleanup short.
Every subprocess runs in its own process group; when it times out, or the oracle
is interrupted while waiting for it, the group gets SIGTERM (CTRL_BREAK on
Windows) and a grace period before it is killed, so acq's and the canary's own
cleanup still runs and no backend command is left running behind them. On
Windows each subprocess also runs in a Job object of its own (``StepJob``): the
kill terminates the job, which holds the step's processes and nothing else, and if
the oracle itself dies the job takes them down with it. A process a step starts with
``CREATE_BREAKAWAY_FROM_JOB`` leaves the job and so survives the kill: the job is
a cleanup tool, not a security boundary.
The harness sends one when a run outlives its timeout, then waits a grace period
before killing it, and exports a deadline (``ACQ_QUALIFY_DEADLINE``, epoch
seconds) that ends before its timeout; the oracle stops starting steps at that
deadline, tears down and reports BLOCKED (a step cut short by the deadline is
BLOCKED, not FAIL). Teardown always tries to remove the nonce-named sandbox,
canary sandbox, secret and provider binding, whether or not this run saw them
created.

Exit codes (mirroring ``scripts/qualify-msb-ro-mount`` and ADR-0044m): 0 PASS,
2 BLOCKED (a prerequisite or a conclusion is missing), 1 FAIL. The throwaway
secret value is ``TIER3_MARKER_<hex>`` and is never printed; captured output is
only searched for it.
"""

from __future__ import annotations

import argparse
import importlib.util
import json
import math
import os
import secrets
import signal
import subprocess
import sys
import tempfile
import time
from dataclasses import dataclass, field
from pathlib import Path
from typing import Callable

# The default shell image every boot-contract cell creates against (the same
# image ``scripts/qualify-msb-ro-mount`` uses).
IMAGE = "docker.io/docker/sandbox-templates:shell-docker"

# Credentials are bound for this host, which can never resolve (RFC 2606 reserves
# ``.invalid``), so nothing can ever be sent to a real server under it.
CRED_HOST = "qualify.invalid"

# Where acq mounts a sandbox's host-config directory read-only in the guest, and
# where it keeps that directory on the host: one ``<sandbox>.<checksum>.config``
# directory per sandbox under its provenance root, as both implementations do
# (``ACQ_HOST_CONFIG_GUEST_DIR`` and ``_acq_provenance_dir`` in
# acq.backends/common.sh, ``state.provenance_dir`` in py-acq). The provenance
# root is ``$XDG_STATE_HOME/acq/provenance``; it does not follow ACQ_STATE_DIR.
HOST_CONFIG_GUEST_DIR = "/var/lib/acq/host"
HOST_CONFIG_GLOB = "{backend}/{sandbox}.*.config"

# A file the pinned usai-provider kit places in the guest (its ``files`` list).
# Its presence shows the built-in kits were applied at create. Update it with the
# kit pin if the kit moves it.
KIT_PROBE_FILE = "/home/agent/usai-config/model-catalog-snapshot.json"

# The first ``create`` pulls the image and boots a microVM; other steps are
# quick. These are the oracle's own per-step budgets (the harness's outer
# ``--timeout`` still bounds the whole run).
CREATE_TIMEOUT = 600.0
STEP_TIMEOUT = 120.0
# The ro-mount canary creates its own sandbox (image pull) and runs several execs.
CANARY_TIMEOUT = 1200.0
# Each teardown command; teardown is not bounded by the deadline.
TEARDOWN_TIMEOUT = 60.0
# The whole run, when the harness does not export a deadline.
DEFAULT_BUDGET = 1500.0
DEADLINE_ENV = "ACQ_QUALIFY_DEADLINE"
# Caller variables the canary would honour but the oracle must pin.
CANARY_PINNED = ("QUALIFY_IMAGE", "EXPECT_SHA", "QUALIFY_SANDBOX_NAME")
# How long a stopped subprocess gets to clean up after SIGTERM before it is killed.
# The canary removes its own sandbox (a 60 s msb call) when stopped.
STOP_GRACE = 30.0
CANARY_GRACE = 90.0
# After a group is killed, how long to wait for its pipes to close. A process that
# left the group (``setsid``, a daemon) can hold them open indefinitely.
REAP_TIMEOUT = 5.0
# A wait on a subprocess returns at least this often, so the oracle's own signal
# handlers run promptly (a Windows wait is not interrupted by SIGBREAK).
WAIT_SLICE = 1.0

# acq's own configuration and state locations, each redirected into the private tree.
ISOLATED_DIRS = (
    "XDG_CONFIG_HOME",
    "XDG_STATE_HOME",
    "XDG_DATA_HOME",
    "XDG_CACHE_HOME",
    "ACQ_STATE_DIR",
    "ACQ_SECRET_STORE_DIR",
)
# Caller ``ACQ_*`` variables that only tune timing and may pass through.
_PASSTHROUGH_SUFFIX = "_TIMEOUT"


def _repo_root() -> Path:
    """The repository root, relative to this script's own location."""
    return Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent


def _load_hostexec():
    """acq's own program resolver, ``acq_py.hostexec``, from this checkout.

    Loaded by file path: this script runs as a plain script, without
    ``py-acq/src`` on ``sys.path``.
    """
    path = _repo_root() / "py-acq" / "src" / "acq_py" / "hostexec.py"
    spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("qualify_backend_cell_hostexec", path)
    if spec is None or spec.loader is None:
        raise ImportError(f"cannot load {path}")
    module = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
    spec.loader.exec_module(module)
    return module


hostexec = _load_hostexec()


def _which_all(name: str, path: str):
    return hostexec.which_all(name, path)


def which(name: str, path: str | None = None) -> str | None:
    """``name`` resolved from the absolute entries of ``PATH`` only (``acq_py.hostexec``).

    An empty, ``.`` or relative entry would resolve against the current directory,
    which may be a workspace a sandboxed agent can write to. ``shutil.which`` is
    not used: on Windows it searches the current directory first even when given
    an explicit path, and accepts batch files through ``PATHEXT``; here only an
    ``.exe`` is accepted there.
    """
    return hostexec.which(name, os.environ.get("PATH", "") if path is None else path)


def _is_wsl_launcher(path: str) -> bool:
    low = path.replace("/", "\\").lower()
    return "\\windows\\system32\\" in low or "\\windowsapps\\" in low


def find_bash(path: str, *, os_name: str = os.name, environ: dict[str, str] | None = None) -> str | None:
    """A bash that can run the canary, or ``None``.

    On Windows ``System32\\bash.exe`` (and the Store alias) is the WSL launcher,
    which would run the canary inside a WSL distribution, not against this host's
    msb. Git Bash is looked for in its install locations first, then any other
    ``bash`` on the absolute ``PATH`` entries.
    """
    if os_name != "nt":
        return which("bash", path)
    environ = os.environ if environ is None else environ
    roots = [environ.get("ProgramFiles"), environ.get("ProgramW6432"), environ.get("ProgramFiles(x86)")]
    local = environ.get("LOCALAPPDATA")
    candidates = [Path(r) / "Git" / "bin" / "bash.exe" for r in roots if r]
    if local:
        candidates.append(Path(local) / "Programs" / "Git" / "bin" / "bash.exe")
    for candidate in candidates:
        if candidate.is_file():
            return str(candidate)
    return next((b for b in _which_all("bash", path) if not _is_wsl_launcher(b)), None)


@dataclass(frozen=True)
class Names:
    """Per-run identifiers: everything that reaches a backend's own store is nonce-scoped."""

    nonce: str
    value: str
    sandbox: str
    service: str
    provider: str
    key_env: str
    secret_env: str
    canary_sandbox: str
    seed: int


def make_names(backend: str, nonce: str | None = None) -> Names:
    nonce = nonce or secrets.token_hex(4)
    return Names(
        nonce=nonce,
        value=f"TIER3_MARKER_{nonce}",
        sandbox=f"acq-qual-{backend}-{nonce}",
        service=f"acqqual{nonce}",
        provider=f"acq-qual-{nonce}",
        key_env=f"ACQ_QUAL_{nonce.upper()}",
        secret_env=f"ACQ_QUAL_S_{nonce.upper()}",
        canary_sandbox=f"acq-qual-ro-{nonce}",
        seed=int(nonce, 16),
    )


def isolated_env(root: Path, base: dict[str, str] | None = None) -> dict[str, str]:
    """The environment every acq (and backend) subprocess runs with; see the module docstring."""
    env = {
        k: v for k, v in (os.environ if base is None else base).items()
        if not k.startswith("ACQ_") or k.endswith(_PASSTHROUGH_SUFFIX)
    }
    for var in ISOLATED_DIRS:
        path = root / var.lower()
        path.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        env[var] = str(path)
    env["PYTHONPATH"] = str(_repo_root() / "py-acq" / "src")
    return env


Run = Callable[..., "subprocess.CompletedProcess[str] | None"]


# Windows: each step's Job object (``StepJob``), and the process-creation flag it
# needs. acq bounds its own backend calls with the same code, so it lives in
# ``acq_py.hostexec``; the step's job holds the step's processes, the oracle's
# own included.
CREATE_SUSPENDED = hostexec.CREATE_SUSPENDED
_BasicLimits, _ExtendedLimits = hostexec.job_structures()
StepJob = hostexec.StepJob


def _signal_group(proc: subprocess.Popen, kill: bool, job: StepJob | None = None) -> None:
    """Signal ``proc``'s whole process group: stop politely, or kill.

    On Windows the kill terminates the step's Job object (``job``).
    """
    if os.name == "nt":
        if not kill:
            proc.send_signal(signal.CTRL_BREAK_EVENT)
        elif job is not None:
            job.terminate()
        else:
            proc.kill()
        return
    try:
        os.killpg(proc.pid, signal.SIGKILL if kill else signal.SIGTERM)
    except (ProcessLookupError, PermissionError):
        pass


def _reap(proc: subprocess.Popen, timeout: float) -> None:
    """Wait at most ``timeout`` seconds for a killed ``proc``, then close its pipes.

    Bounded because a process outside the killed group may still hold the pipes.
    """
    try:
        proc.communicate(timeout=timeout)
    except (subprocess.TimeoutExpired, OSError, ValueError):
        pass
    for pipe in (proc.stdin, proc.stdout, proc.stderr):
        if pipe is not None:
            try:
                pipe.close()
            except OSError:
                pass
    try:
        proc.wait(timeout=timeout)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        pass


def stop_process(proc: subprocess.Popen, grace: float, job: StepJob | None = None) -> None:
    """SIGTERM (CTRL_BREAK on Windows) to the group, ``grace`` seconds, then kill the group.

    On Windows the kill terminates ``job``, the step's Job object.
    """
    try:
        _signal_group(proc, kill=False)
        proc.communicate(timeout=grace)
    except (subprocess.TimeoutExpired, OSError):
        _signal_group(proc, kill=True, job=job)
        _reap(proc, min(REAP_TIMEOUT, grace))


def _communicate(proc: subprocess.Popen, stdin: str | None, timeout: float):
    """``proc.communicate(stdin, timeout=timeout)``, waiting in ``WAIT_SLICE`` slices.

    Retrying after ``TimeoutExpired`` loses no output (documented ``subprocess``
    behaviour). ``stdin`` goes only to the first call: the input is a short
    credential value, which fits the pipe buffer and is written whole at once.
    """
    end = time.monotonic() + timeout
    while True:
        remaining = end - time.monotonic()
        try:
            return proc.communicate(stdin, timeout=max(0.0, min(remaining, WAIT_SLICE)))
        except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
            if remaining <= WAIT_SLICE:
                raise
            stdin = None


def run_process(argv: list[str], *, env: dict[str, str], stdin: str | None = None,
                timeout: float = STEP_TIMEOUT, cwd: str | None = None, grace: float = STOP_GRACE):
    """Run ``argv`` capturing its output; ``None`` on a timeout or if it cannot start.

    It runs in its own process group, and the wait on it is sliced so a stop
    signal to the oracle is handled within ``WAIT_SLICE`` seconds. On a timeout, or when the oracle itself is
    interrupted (SIGTERM, Ctrl+C) while waiting, the group is stopped with
    :func:`stop_process`, so a backend command it started (an ``msb create``, the
    canary's sandbox) is not left running and its own cleanup gets to run; a plain
    ``subprocess.run`` timeout would SIGKILL only the direct child. On Windows it
    also runs in a :class:`StepJob`, which the kill terminates and which is
    released once the step is over.
    """
    started = _spawn(argv, env=env, cwd=cwd, stdin=stdin is not None)
    if started is None:
        return None
    proc, job = started
    try:
        out, err = _communicate(proc, stdin, timeout)
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        stop_process(proc, grace, job)
        return None
    except BaseException:
        # The oracle itself is being stopped, and that stop is already on its
        # way out. A second stop signal (a terminal's Ctrl+C reaches the oracle
        # as SIGINT and the harness forwards a SIGTERM as well) must not cut
        # this step's stop short and leave the step running. The oracle's own
        # stop handlers already swallow it (``_first_stop``); this also covers
        # an interrupt that did not come through them.
        previous = _ignore_stop_signals()
        try:
            stop_process(proc, grace, job)
        finally:
            _restore_signals(previous)
        raise
    finally:
        if job is not None:
            job.release()
    return subprocess.CompletedProcess(list(argv), proc.returncode, out, err)


def _spawn(argv: list[str], *, env: dict[str, str], cwd: str | None, stdin: bool):
    """Start ``argv`` in its own process group (and, on Windows, its own Job object).

    Returns ``(proc, job)``, ``job`` being ``None`` off Windows, or ``None`` if it
    cannot start. On Windows the process is created suspended and resumed only
    once it is in the job; if that fails it is killed instead, so a step never
    runs where it could not be stopped completely.
    """
    windows = os.name == "nt"
    group = ({"creationflags": subprocess.CREATE_NEW_PROCESS_GROUP | CREATE_SUSPENDED} if windows
             else {"start_new_session": True})
    try:
        proc = subprocess.Popen(list(argv), env=env, cwd=cwd, text=True, errors="replace",
                                stdin=subprocess.PIPE if stdin else subprocess.DEVNULL,
                                stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.PIPE, **group)
    except OSError:
        return None
    if not windows:
        return proc, None
    try:
        return proc, StepJob.adopt(proc.pid)
    except BaseException as exc:
        # A stop signal that lands here must not leave a suspended process behind.
        proc.kill()
        _reap(proc, REAP_TIMEOUT)
        if isinstance(exc, OSError):
            return None
        raise


def canary_env(env: dict[str, str], names: Names) -> dict[str, str]:
    """The canary's environment: the oracle's, with its image pinned and this run's sandbox name."""
    pinned = {k: v for k, v in env.items() if k not in CANARY_PINNED}
    pinned["QUALIFY_SANDBOX_NAME"] = names.canary_sandbox
    return pinned


def _out(proc) -> str:
    return "" if proc is None else (proc.stdout or "") + (proc.stderr or "")


def snapshot(root: Path | None) -> dict[str, bytes] | None:
    """Every file under ``root`` with its bytes, or ``None`` if it cannot be read."""
    if root is None:
        return None
    try:
        return {str(p.relative_to(root)): p.read_bytes() for p in sorted(root.rglob("*")) if p.is_file()}
    except OSError:
        return None


class DeadlineReached(Exception):
    """The run's time budget ran out before a step could start."""


def _problem(proc) -> str | None:
    """Why a completed step failed, or ``None`` if it exited 0."""
    if proc is None:
        return "timed out or could not start"
    if proc.returncode != 0:
        return f"exit {proc.returncode}"
    return None


@dataclass
class Cell:
    """One oracle run for one backend. ``acq`` and ``raw`` are injectable for tests.

    ``acq(argv, stdin=None, timeout=...)`` runs the shipped CLI; ``raw(argv, ...)``
    runs a program directly; ``canary(timeout)`` runs the ro-mount canary and
    returns ``(exit status, stdout)`` (status ``None`` when no usable ``bash``
    exists or it timed out); ``state_dir`` is acq's private state root, where its
    sandbox records are read, and ``provenance_dir`` its private provenance root,
    where host-config directories are read. Steps stop starting at ``deadline``
    (epoch seconds).
    """

    backend: str
    names: Names
    workspace: str
    state_dir: Path
    acq: Run
    raw: Run
    canary: Callable[[float], "tuple[int | None, str]"]
    deadline: float = math.inf
    provenance_dir: Path | None = None
    failures: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
    blocked: list[str] = field(default_factory=list)
    bound: bool = False

    # -- reporting -------------------------------------------------------------

    def fail(self, step: str, detail: str) -> None:
        self.failures.append(step)
        print(f"FAIL: {step}: {detail}", file=sys.stderr)

    def block(self, step: str, detail: str) -> None:
        self.blocked.append(step)
        print(f"BLOCKED: {step}: {detail}", file=sys.stderr)

    def check(self, step: str, proc) -> bool:
        """Record a failure if ``proc`` failed or its output carries the secret value."""
        if self.names.value in _out(proc):
            self.fail(step, "the secret value appeared in captured output")
            return False
        problem = _problem(proc)
        if problem:
            self.fail(step, problem)
            return False
        return True

    def _b(self, *argv: str) -> list[str]:
        return ["--backend", self.backend, *argv]

    def budget(self, timeout: float) -> float:
        """``timeout`` cut to what is left before the deadline; raise if nothing is."""
        remaining = self.deadline - time.time()
        if remaining <= 1:
            raise DeadlineReached
        return min(timeout, remaining)

    def call(self, argv: list[str], *, stdin: str | None = None, timeout: float = STEP_TIMEOUT):
        proc = self.acq(argv, stdin=stdin, timeout=self.budget(timeout))
        self._deadline_check(proc)
        return proc

    def _deadline_check(self, result) -> None:
        """A step that got no result because the deadline cut it short is BLOCKED, not FAIL."""
        if result is None and time.time() >= self.deadline - 1:
            raise DeadlineReached

    # -- steps ------------------------------------------------------------------

    def bind_credential(self) -> None:
        """oracle 2: bind a throwaway credential before create (value on stdin only)."""
        n = self.names
        proc = self.call(self._b("provider-credential", "set", n.provider, n.key_env, "--host", CRED_HOST, "--json"),
                         stdin=n.value + "\n")
        self.bound = proc is not None and proc.returncode == 0
        if self.check("provider-credential set", proc):
            self.check("provider-credential ls", self.call(self._b("provider-credential", "ls", "--json")))
        if self.backend == "msb":
            # The default usai provider kit is selected, and acq refuses an msb
            # create whose provider has no stored key (msb binds keys only at
            # create). The throwaway value lives in this run's isolated store.
            # On sbx acq checks sbx's own table instead, which this script never
            # writes globally: main() preflights it (see the module docstring).
            self.check("secret set usai", self.call(self._b("secret", "set", "-g", "usai", "--json"),
                                                    stdin=n.value + "\n"))

    def boot(self) -> bool:
        """oracle 3: create/ls/exec. Returns False (and stops the run) on any failure."""
        n = self.names
        created = self.call(self._b("create", "shell", self.workspace, "--name", n.sandbox, "--image", IMAGE, "--json"),
                            timeout=CREATE_TIMEOUT)
        if not self.check("create", created):
            return False
        listed = self.call(self._b("ls", "--json"))
        if not self.check("ls", listed):
            return False
        try:
            sandboxes = json.loads(listed.stdout).get("data", {}).get("sandboxes", [])
            names = [e.get("name") for e in sandboxes if isinstance(e, dict)]
        except (json.JSONDecodeError, AttributeError):
            self.fail("ls", "unparseable listing")
            return False
        if n.sandbox not in names:
            self.fail("ls", f"sandbox '{n.sandbox}' not present after create")
            return False
        script = f"printf 'boot=%s\\n' \"$(({n.seed} + 3))\""
        return self.guest_token("exec", "boot", n.seed + 3, script) is not None

    def guest(self, script: str):
        return self.call(self._b("exec", self.names.sandbox, "--", "sh", "-c", script))

    def guest_token(self, step: str, key: str, expected: int, script: str) -> str | None:
        """Run ``script`` in the guest; return its output if it printed ``key=<expected>``.

        The number is computed in the guest, so a backend that echoes the command
        line instead of running it does not produce it.
        """
        proc = self.guest(script)
        if not self.check(step, proc):
            return None
        out = _out(proc)
        if f"{key}={expected}" not in out.splitlines():
            self.fail(step, "the guest did not run the probe (its computed result is missing)")
            return None
        return out

    def guest_credential(self) -> None:
        """oracle 2: in the guest, a placeholder is bound and the value is nowhere in the environment."""
        if not self.bound:
            self.fail("guest credential", "no credential was bound (provider-credential set failed)")
            return
        n = self.names
        pid1 = f"/tmp/acq-qual-pid1-{n.nonce}"
        script = (
            f"printf 'cred=%s\\n' \"$(({n.seed} + 1))\"; "
            f"printf 'placeholder=%s\\n' \"${{{n.key_env}:-}}\"; "
            "env; "
            f"if cat /proc/1/environ > {pid1} 2>/dev/null || sudo -n cat /proc/1/environ > {pid1} 2>/dev/null; "
            f"then tr '\\000' '\\n' < {pid1}; printf 'pid1=readable\\n'; else printf 'pid1=unreadable\\n'; fi; "
            f"rm -f {pid1}"
        )
        out = self.guest_token("guest credential", "cred", n.seed + 1, script)
        if out is None:
            return
        lines = out.splitlines()
        placeholder = next((x.split("=", 1)[1] for x in lines if x.startswith("placeholder=")), "")
        if not placeholder:
            self.fail("guest credential", f"{n.key_env} is not set in the guest; the credential was not bound")
        if "pid1=readable" not in lines:
            self.block("guest credential", "/proc/1/environ is unreadable (even with sudo -n), so PID 1's "
                       "environment was not checked for the value")

    def kits(self) -> None:
        """oracle 3: the built-in kits were applied at create."""
        script = f"test -s {KIT_PROBE_FILE} && printf 'kit=%s\\n' \"$(({self.names.seed} + 2))\"; true"
        proc = self.guest(script)
        if self.check("kit apply", proc) and f"kit={self.names.seed + 2}" not in _out(proc).splitlines():
            self.fail("kit apply", f"{KIT_PROBE_FILE} (placed by the usai-provider kit) is missing in the guest")

    def host_config_dir(self) -> Path | None:
        if self.provenance_dir is None:
            return None
        found = sorted(self.provenance_dir.glob(HOST_CONFIG_GLOB.format(backend=self.backend, sandbox=self.names.sandbox)))
        return found[0] if len(found) == 1 and found[0].is_dir() else None

    def ro_mount(self) -> None:
        """oracle 1 (msb): the trusted-config mount is read-only in this guest; acq records it; msb enforces ``:ro``."""
        n = self.names
        records = sorted((self.state_dir / self.backend).glob(f"{n.sandbox}.*.json"))
        try:
            record = json.loads(records[0].read_text(encoding="utf-8")) if records else None
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            record = None
        if not (isinstance(record, dict) and record.get("ro_host_config") is True):
            self.fail("ro mount record", "acq's sandbox record does not declare the read-only trusted-config mount")
        self.ro_effect()
        status, out = self.canary(self.budget(CANARY_TIMEOUT))
        self._deadline_check(status)
        # The verdict needs the canary's own RESULT line, not just an exit status.
        verdict = next((x.split()[1] for x in out.splitlines() if x.startswith("RESULT ") and len(x.split()) > 1), "")
        if status == 1 and verdict == "FAIL":
            self.fail("ro-mount canary", "scripts/qualify-msb-ro-mount reported a write through the :ro volume")
        elif not (status == 0 and verdict == "PASS"):
            reason = "no usable bash, or it timed out" if status is None else f"exit {status}, RESULT {verdict or 'missing'}"
            self.block("ro-mount canary", f"no conclusion ({reason})")

    def ro_effect(self) -> None:
        """In this cell's own guest: ``ro`` in /proc/mounts, a write fails with EROFS, the host is unchanged."""
        n = self.names
        host = self.host_config_dir()
        before = snapshot(host)
        target = f"{HOST_CONFIG_GUEST_DIR}/.acq-qual-{n.nonce}"
        err = f"/tmp/acq-qual-err-{n.nonce}"
        script = (
            f"printf 'ro=%s\\n' \"$(({n.seed} + 4))\"; "
            f"awk '$2 == \"{HOST_CONFIG_GUEST_DIR}\" {{ print \"opts=\" $4 }}' /proc/mounts; "
            "if sudo -n true 2>/dev/null; then w='sudo -n'; printf 'as=root\\n'; else w=; printf 'as=user\\n'; fi; "
            f"if $w sh -c 'echo x > \"$1\"' sh {target} 2>{err}; then printf 'write=ok\\n'; "
            f"else printf 'write=denied:%s\\n' \"$(head -c 300 {err} | tr '\\n' ' ')\"; fi; rm -f {err}"
        )
        out = self.guest_token("ro mount", "ro", n.seed + 4, script)
        if out is None:
            return
        lines = out.splitlines()
        opts = [x[len("opts="):].split(",") for x in lines if x.startswith("opts=")]
        if not opts:
            self.fail("ro mount", f"nothing is mounted at {HOST_CONFIG_GUEST_DIR} in the guest")
        elif "ro" not in opts[-1]:
            self.fail("ro mount", f"{HOST_CONFIG_GUEST_DIR} is mounted read-write in the guest")
        write = next((x for x in lines if x.startswith("write=")), "write=")
        if write == "write=ok":
            self.fail("ro mount", f"the guest wrote to {HOST_CONFIG_GUEST_DIR}")
        elif "Read-only file system" not in write:
            self.fail("ro mount", f"the write to {HOST_CONFIG_GUEST_DIR} did not fail with EROFS")
        if host is None:
            self.fail("ro mount", "acq keeps no host-config directory for the sandbox "
                      f"(expected <provenance>/{HOST_CONFIG_GLOB.format(backend=self.backend, sandbox=n.sandbox)})")
        elif before is None or snapshot(host) != before:
            self.fail("ro mount", "the host-config directory changed while the guest tried to write to it")

    def remove(self) -> None:
        self.check("rm", self.call(self._b("rm", self.names.sandbox, "--json")))

    def _secret_set_argv(self) -> list[str]:
        n = self.names
        # A non-built-in service is bound only with --host/--env on both backends
        # (AGENTS.md): msb binds ENV@HOST, sbx uses `secret set-custom`.
        return self._b("secret", "set", "-g", n.service, "--host", CRED_HOST, "--env", n.secret_env, "--json")

    def secret_round_trip(self) -> None:
        """oracle 2: host-side ``secret set/ls/rm`` never print the value (nonce-named service)."""
        n = self.names
        if not self.check("secret set", self.call(self._secret_set_argv(), stdin=n.value + "\n")):
            return
        self.check("secret ls", self.call(self._b("secret", "ls", "--json")))
        self.check("secret rm", self.call(self._b("secret", "rm", "-g", n.service, "--json")))

    def unbind_credential(self) -> None:
        if self.bound:
            self.check("provider-credential rm",
                       self.call(self._b("provider-credential", "rm", self.names.provider, "--json")))

    def run(self) -> int:
        n = self.names
        print(f"qualify-backend-cell: backend={self.backend} sandbox={n.sandbox} secret-service={n.service} "
              f"provider={n.provider} env={n.key_env},{n.secret_env}", file=sys.stderr)
        try:
            self.bind_credential()
            if not self.boot():
                return 1
            self.guest_credential()
            self.kits()
            if self.backend == "msb":
                self.ro_mount()
            self.remove()
            self.secret_round_trip()
            self.unbind_credential()
        except DeadlineReached:
            self.block("deadline", "the run's time budget ran out; remaining steps were not run")
        finally:
            self.teardown()
        if self.failures:
            return 1
        if self.blocked:
            return 2
        print(f"qualify-backend-cell: PASS backend={self.backend}")
        return 0

    def teardown(self) -> None:
        """Best effort: remove everything this run may have created, and nothing else.

        Every identifier is nonce-scoped, so each removal is attempted whether or
        not this run saw the thing created: an abort can land between a backend
        creating it and acq reporting that it did.

        A stop signal (the harness's timeout SIGTERM, a second Ctrl+C, SIGBREAK)
        is swallowed meanwhile, so it cannot cut the removals short. Teardown is
        bounded by its per-step timeouts, and the harness still kills the oracle
        once its grace period runs out.
        """
        previous = _ignore_stop_signals()
        try:
            self._remove_everything()
        finally:
            _restore_signals(previous)

    def _remove_everything(self) -> None:
        n = self.names
        for argv in (self._b("rm", n.sandbox, "--json"),
                     self._b("secret", "rm", "-g", n.service, "--json"),
                     self._b("provider-credential", "rm", n.provider, "--json")):
            self.acq(argv, timeout=TEARDOWN_TIMEOUT)
        if self.backend == "msb":
            # The canary removes its own sandbox, unless it was killed first.
            self.raw(["msb", "remove", "--force", n.canary_sandbox], timeout=TEARDOWN_TIMEOUT)
        if self.backend == "sbx":
            # ``provider-credential rm`` removes the custom secret it created, but
            # an aborted run or a failed removal can leave one. Sweep for it, found
            # by this run's nonce-named variables, never by a fixed name.
            remove_sbx_custom_secrets(self.raw, {n.key_env, n.secret_env})


USAI_KEY_ENV = "USAI_API_KEY"
USAI_HOST = "api.gsa.usai.gov"


def sbx_usai_key_present(raw: Run) -> bool | None:
    """True when sbx's global table holds a USAI_API_KEY custom secret for the
    USAi host, False when it does not, None when the listing cannot be read.
    Reads names and targets only; ``secret`` is masked by sbx."""
    proc = raw(["sbx", "secret", "ls", "--json"], timeout=STEP_TIMEOUT)
    try:
        doc = json.loads(proc.stdout) if proc is not None and proc.returncode == 0 else None
    except (json.JSONDecodeError, TypeError):
        return None
    custom = doc.get("custom_secrets") if isinstance(doc, dict) else None
    if not isinstance(custom, list):
        return None
    return any(
        isinstance(e, dict) and e.get("scope") == "global" and e.get("env") == USAI_KEY_ENV
        and USAI_HOST in (e.get("targets") or [])
        for e in custom
    )


def remove_sbx_custom_secrets(raw: Run, key_envs: set[str]) -> None:
    """Remove the sbx custom secrets whose ``env`` is one of ``key_envs``.

    Field shape (verified live against sbx 0.51.0): a ``custom_secrets`` entry is
    ``{"scope", "targets", "env", "placeholder", "secret": "<masked>"}``; it is
    removed by ``placeholder``. Never reads a value (``secret`` is masked);
    failures are swallowed (teardown only). ``--force`` skips sbx's interactive
    confirmation.
    """
    proc = raw(["sbx", "secret", "ls", "--json"], timeout=TEARDOWN_TIMEOUT)
    try:
        doc = json.loads(proc.stdout) if proc is not None and proc.returncode == 0 else None
    except (json.JSONDecodeError, TypeError):
        return
    custom = doc.get("custom_secrets") if isinstance(doc, dict) else None
    for entry in custom if isinstance(custom, list) else []:
        if not isinstance(entry, dict):
            continue
        placeholder = entry.get("placeholder")
        if entry.get("env") in key_envs and isinstance(placeholder, str) and placeholder:
            raw(["sbx", "secret", "rm", "--placeholder", placeholder, "--force"], timeout=TEARDOWN_TIMEOUT)


def _deadline(environ) -> float:
    raw = environ.get(DEADLINE_ENV, "")
    try:
        # float() also accepts other scripts' digits; only an ASCII deadline is valid.
        value = float(raw) if raw.isascii() else math.nan
    except ValueError:
        value = math.nan
    return value if math.isfinite(value) else time.time() + DEFAULT_BUDGET


def _first_stop() -> None:
    """Swallow every further stop signal from now on; called by the first stop handler.

    It runs inside the handler, before the handler raises, so there is no window
    in which a second stop (a terminal's Ctrl+C reaches the oracle as SIGINT and
    the harness forwards a SIGTERM as well) can land while the first is still
    unwinding, before a step's stop or teardown sets up its own guard, and cut
    that cleanup short. The oracle is on its way out by then; the harness still
    kills it once its grace period runs out.
    """
    _ignore_stop_signals()


def _terminate(signum, _frame):
    _first_stop()
    raise SystemExit(128 + signum)


def _interrupt(_signum, _frame):
    """SIGINT: ``KeyboardInterrupt``, as Python's default handler, without the window."""
    _first_stop()
    raise KeyboardInterrupt


def _install_stop_handlers() -> None:
    """Turn SIGTERM, SIGBREAK (Windows) and SIGINT into a normal exit that tears down.

    SIGBREAK is what reaches a Windows console process group. SIGINT is taken
    over only from Python's default handler: one that was ignored when the
    oracle started (a background job) stays ignored.
    """
    signal.signal(signal.SIGTERM, _terminate)
    if hasattr(signal, "SIGBREAK"):
        signal.signal(signal.SIGBREAK, _terminate)
    if signal.getsignal(signal.SIGINT) is signal.default_int_handler:
        signal.signal(signal.SIGINT, _interrupt)


def _swallow(_signum, _frame) -> None:
    """A no-op handler.

    Used rather than ``SIG_IGN``: an ignored signal stays ignored across
    ``exec`` on POSIX, so teardown's own subprocesses would then ignore the
    SIGTERM :func:`stop_process` sends them. A caught one is reset in the child.
    """


def _stop_signals() -> list[int]:
    return [getattr(signal, name) for name in ("SIGINT", "SIGTERM", "SIGBREAK") if hasattr(signal, name)]


def _ignore_stop_signals() -> dict[int, object]:
    """Swallow SIGINT, SIGTERM and (Windows) SIGBREAK; return the handlers to restore."""
    previous = {}
    for sig in _stop_signals():
        try:
            previous[sig] = signal.signal(sig, _swallow)
        except (ValueError, OSError):  # not the main thread
            pass
    return previous


def _restore_signals(previous: dict[int, object]) -> None:
    for sig, handler in previous.items():
        try:
            signal.signal(sig, handler if handler is not None else signal.SIG_DFL)
        except (ValueError, OSError, TypeError):
            pass


def main(argv: list[str] | None = None) -> int:
    parser = argparse.ArgumentParser(
        prog="qualify-backend-cell",
        description="Per-cell Tier-3 qualification oracle (ADR-0044m): boot contract, credential binding "
        "and non-exposure, kit apply, and (msb) the read-only trusted-config mount.",
    )
    parser.add_argument("--backend", required=True, choices=["msb", "sbx"], help="backend to qualify")
    backend: str = parser.parse_args(argv).backend

    # --- BLOCKED preconditions (exit 2). ---
    if sys.version_info < (3, 11):  # ``python -P``
        print("BLOCKED: Python >= 3.11 is required", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    exe = which(backend)
    if exe is None:
        print(f"BLOCKED: {backend} CLI is not on PATH", file=sys.stderr)
        return 2
    names = make_names(backend)
    deadline = _deadline(os.environ)
    # A terminated or interrupted run must still tear down.
    _install_stop_handlers()
    # A Windows file still held open by a just-stopped process must not turn the
    # verdict into a traceback.
    with tempfile.TemporaryDirectory(prefix="acq-qual-", ignore_cleanup_errors=True) as tmp:
        root = Path(tmp)
        env = isolated_env(root / "acq")
        workspace = root / "workspace"
        workspace.mkdir()
        version = run_process([exe, "--version" if backend == "msb" else "version"], env=env, timeout=15.0)
        if version is None or version.returncode != 0:
            print(f"BLOCKED: {backend} CLI did not run", file=sys.stderr)
            return 2
        python = [sys.executable, "-P", "-s", "-m", "acq_py"]

        def acq(args, *, stdin=None, timeout=STEP_TIMEOUT):
            return run_process([*python, *args], env=env, stdin=stdin, timeout=timeout, cwd=str(root))

        def raw(args, *, stdin=None, timeout=STEP_TIMEOUT):
            resolved = which(args[0], env.get("PATH", ""))
            if resolved is None:
                return None
            return run_process([resolved, *args[1:]], env=env, stdin=stdin, timeout=timeout, cwd=str(root))

        def canary(timeout):
            bash = find_bash(env.get("PATH", ""))
            if bash is None:
                return None, ""
            proc = run_process([bash, str(_repo_root() / "scripts" / "qualify-msb-ro-mount")],
                               env=canary_env(env, names), timeout=timeout, cwd=str(_repo_root()),
                               grace=CANARY_GRACE)
            return (None, "") if proc is None else (proc.returncode, proc.stdout or "")

        if backend == "sbx":
            present = sbx_usai_key_present(raw)
            if not present:
                why = "cannot read 'sbx secret ls --json'" if present is None else (
                    f"sbx holds no global {USAI_KEY_ENV} secret for {USAI_HOST}")
                print(f"BLOCKED: usai key: {why}; acq refuses the create without it "
                      "(store one with 'acq --backend sbx secret set -g usai')", file=sys.stderr)
                return 2
        cell = Cell(backend, names, str(workspace), Path(env["ACQ_STATE_DIR"]), acq, raw, canary, deadline,
                    provenance_dir=Path(env["XDG_STATE_HOME"]) / "acq" / "provenance")
        return cell.run()


if __name__ == "__main__":
    raise SystemExit(main())
